Jamie (Customer): Hey, hitting 400s when calling the tool via Redwood API. Error: "Invalid tool args: unexpected control character at 42". This started after we upgraded the client.

Jamie (Customer): minimal curl we use: 
```bash
curl -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/call \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN' \
  -H 'Content-Type: text/plain' \
  --data-binary '{"tool":"read_file","args":"{\'path\': 'C:\\new\\report.txt', \nline: 'first line\nsecond line'}"}'
```

CaseBot: Thanks Jamie — ticket created: RB-4921. We'll triage. :white_check_mark:

Maria (Support): Can you paste the full request_id from the 400 response (request_id header) and the exact response body? Also which client version are you on?

Jamie (Customer): request_id: rq_2026-11-03_0a5f9c. Response body: `{"error":"Invalid tool args: unexpected control character at 42"}`. Client is a small wrapper around fetch we wrote, v1.3.0.

Alex (Eng Oncall): Thanks — quick suspicion: your Content-Type is `text/plain` and you're passing a string that itself contains JSON-like content with single quotes and unescaped backslashes/newlines. Redwood expects the `args` field to be valid JSON (application/json) for tool calling. When content-type is text/plain we treat payload as raw text and then attempt to parse tool args which leads to control-char errors.

Sanjay (Eng-ML): Another angle: inside your `args` you have `"{\'path\': 'C:\\\\new\\\\report.txt', line: 'first line\nsecond line' }"` — that's a JS/Python-like literal (single quotes, unquoted key `line`) and an embedded newline. JSON parser will choke.

Jamie (Customer): Oh — we were double-encoding on purpose to support legacy infra. We thought wrapping the inner JSON as a string would be ok.

Maria (Support): For a quick repro, can you try sending a proper JSON payload and `Content-Type: application/json`? Example (works on our side):

```bash
curl -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/call \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  --data '{
    "tool": "read_file",
    "args": {
      "path": "C:\\new\\report.txt",
      "line": "first line\\nsecond line"
    }
  }'
```

Alex (Eng Oncall): If you must keep the inner args as a string (not recommended), you need to escape control chars and backslashes properly, e.g. "line": "first line\\nsecond line" and "path": "C:\\\\new\\\\report.txt". But best practice: send `args` as an object so we don't try to re-parse a string.

Jamie (Customer): Confirmed switching to `application/json` and passing `args` as an object fixed it. No 400s now. Thanks!

Sanjay (Eng-ML): Filing a small docs tweak to call out common pitfalls: - content-type must be application/json for tool calls; - args must be valid JSON; - show Windows path escaping examples and newline examples.

Maria (Support): Marking RB-4921 as resolved pending your confirmation. If you need a migration snippet we can send a small helper to convert your old double-encoded payloads. :sparkles:

Jamie (Customer): Please send the helper snippet — would save us some time. Appreciate the quick turnaround.

Alex (Eng Oncall): Will send a tiny JS helper in the thread:
```js
// convert legacy double-encoded args -> proper payload
function normalize(payloadString) {
  // payloadString is the raw body you used to send as text/plain
  try {
    const parsed = JSON.parse(payloadString); // outer JSON
    if (typeof parsed.args === 'string') {
      parsed.args = JSON.parse(parsed.args.replace(/\\r\\n|\\r/g, '\\n'));
    }
    return parsed;
  } catch (e) {
    throw new Error('failed to normalize payload: ' + e.message);
  }
}
```

Maria (Support): Closing loop: we'll add a short note to the troubleshooting docs and include the helper. Thanks everyone. :raised_hands:
